11. Tell us about a time you fixed a production bug under pressure.

    In one of our production systems, we had a microservice running on Amazon ECS with AWS Fargate that was consuming
    events from Apache Kafka and writing processed data to our data lake on Amazon S3.

    🔹 Context
    - High-throughput event pipeline
    - 50K–100K events per minute
    - ECS Fargate tasks consuming Kafka partitions
    - Processed data stored in S3 (partitioned by date/hour)

    🔹 Problem
    One day, we noticed:
    - ECS Fargate CPU utilization > 90%
    - Kafka consumer lag growing beyond 50 million messages
    - Frequent ECS task restarts
    - Metrics showing discrepancies compared to historical baselines

    🔹 Impact
    - Data pipeline delay of ~30–40 minutes
    - Increased pressure on Kafka cluster due to backlog

    1️⃣ Checked Infrastructure Metrics
    - CPU & memory in CloudWatch
    - Task exit reasons
    - OOMKilled events
    - Scaling activity logs
    - Observation:
        - Tasks were restarting due to memory pressure.
        - CPU constantly above 90%.
        - Auto-scaling was slow to react.

    2️⃣ Checked Kafka Metrics
    - Consumer lag per partition
    - Rebalance frequency
    - Partition assignment
    - Observation:
        - Frequent task restarts triggered Kafka rebalancing.
        - During rebalance, consumers paused → lag spiked even more.
        - A rebalance storm pattern was visible.

    3️⃣ Issue found with Opensearch Data Write
    - Opensearch was not functioning properly
    - For one of the cluster storage was available only < 20GB
    - Opensearch Cluster health status as Yellow
    - Instance Type General Purpose
        - r7g.large.search - master nodes - 3 data nodes
        - m7g.large.search - data nodes - EBS - 3 data nodes

    Instance Types
        Dev: cache.t3.micro (2 nodes)
        QA/Stg: cache.t3.small (2 nodes)
        Prod: cache.t4g.small (2 nodes, ARM-based)

        Ingestion / Parquet-loader = 2 vCPU/ 4G Memory

        data-signal-processor - parallellism 8, Parallelism per KPU 2, 4 KPU, One KPU represents 1 vCPU, 4 GiB of memory, and 50 GiB of running

        kafka -
            kafka.m7g.large
            kafka.m7g.2xlarge
            kafka.m7g.xlarge
            kafka.m5.2xlarge

